three of germany's political parties  have struck a deal to govern together  despite their differences the so-called  traffic light coalition named after the  party colours includes  the center-left demo social democrats  the greens and the liberal fdp they aim  to strengthen germany's role on the  international stage and do more to  tackle the climate crisis now the  parties have agreed to introduce a 12  euro minimum wage the coalition wants to  phase out coal by 2030 they also want to  legalize the sale of cannabis which  could bring in billions of euro in texas  and they aim to build 400 000 new homes  a year  to ease a housing shortage  now here's a look at how the day  unfolded in berlin  for the first time since the social  democrats won the election two months  ago germans were given a clear roadmap  for the future  three parties who have never governed  together have reached a lengthy  agreement on how to run europe's largest  economy despite their differences  we're not interested in the politics of  the lowest common denominator instead  we're interested in great impacts we  want to have the ambition to do  something about climate protection about  the restructuring of our industry about  the modernization of the country about  the strengthening of social cohesion  we want to dare to achieve more progress  perhaps the biggest announcement was the  formation of a new ministry combining  climate and the economy to be headed by  robert habeck of the greens  no longer a fringe issue the new  coalition promises to put the climate at  the forefront of its policy  considerations  modernizing a country in a time of  crisis means a huge effort and a great  deal of determination will be needed  we've now laid the basis for this effort  and determination  at the heart of this new story that we  can write together is the compatibility  of prosperity and climate protection  but that is not the only crisis  threatening germany and the european  union  the incoming government says it wants to  put european foreign policy first in  dealing with problems in its own  backyard  we're tackling this common challenge at  a time when the crisis in our immediate  vicinity here in the middle of europe  are becoming more severe  the challenges are becoming more  difficult  if we look at the situation in belarus  or the situation at europe's external  borders that is why we have agreed  together to return to an active european  foreign policy  the agreement addressed domestic needs  as well such as affordable housing the  need to improve germany's vaccination  rate as another coveted winter  approaches  and digital  the younger generation in particular has  given us a mandate to overcome this  status quo of the past years  schultz is expected to be named  chancellor on december 8th  then it will be up to him and the rest  of the coalition to put the plan into  action  let's bring in our chief uh political  editor michelle kufner for more uh  michelle it's clear that the new  government coalition partners uh they've  got ambitious plans to to change germany  into a greener more efficient and  progressive society do you have any  better ideas about how they'll put that  into action  well certainly on the key issue of  climate they are creating a super  ministry  a trade and climate ministry that will  be headed by the green co leader robert  harbeck  and that will  bundle a lot more competencies but  behind all of that speak is still the  big question where the money will be put  and that's firmly in the hands of the  pro-business free democrats and that's  where we have the inbuilt divide right  there we don't know how much will be  earmarked in the new budget for which  ministry at the same time  we heard a lot less writing and talk  about international ambitions than in  the past agreement and at that press  conference that's what i put to the  incoming chancellor olaf choice just  let's take a look at that clip  for an international audience in english  if i may mr schultz  during the last coalition you were vice  chancellor europe was at the very top of  that last coalition deal now foreign  policy starts at page 142.  how will  german foreign policy change  and where will you start as chancellor  or maybe also if you want to mention who  the foreign minister will be  deutsche  germany stands by its responsibility  within the framework of the european  union  we're a large country in the middle of  europe that's committed to the task of  making the european union better every  day  making it work better  we are not standing on the sidelines  commenting but we want to play our part  in making a strong sovereign europe  possible  and we are aware of our deep  transatlantic ties in particular our  cooperation with the us  this will be central to our future  government  michelle i want to ask you about the uh  the contest for cabinet positions during  the negotiations what does it um tell  you about how how different  these parties really are  well it tells me that some tough  decisions had to be made for instance  the transport ministry didn't go to the  greens that's something they're already  getting quite a bit of fire over from  their own party base and they will have  to put this whole marriage contract of  the coalition to the party grassroots  that will be continuing over the coming  days and the week after next we will  then most likely  see olaf shorts sworn in and we're still  to learn who the names are on the social  democrat side  they are the strongest party that is  also reflected but we saw the weakest  party get four of those ministries at  least two can be considered key  that demonstrates that a delicate  balance here is being struck as this  coalition already heads into its first  baptism of fire which is the current  covert crisis here in germany and  growing public frustration that  politicians both the current caretaker  government and the incoming coalition  are too slow and not effective enough at  responding to this michelle it's been 16  years of angela merkel olive schultz is  set to become chancellor you've reported  on both of them extensively but just  tell us a little bit about what kind of  a leader olive schultz is going to be  well he still is  angela mackel's vice chancellor in the  coalition he has learnt from angela  merkel how to do chancellor to a certain  degree um people close to him say that  he's reliable but he's also good at  affording compromises and sticking to  them he is a rather softly spoken man  you saw in that clip there he doesn't  come across as sparkly or charismatic as  some other world leaders but he  certainly knows a lot of them and he  just won an election by coming across  rather macholesce so i think it's the  maximum continuity one can expect from  the other side of that current coalition  all right that's dw's chief political  editor talking to us there thank you  michelle as always
 

